# What the Ventoy installer UKI adds to installer.cmdline (make-installer.sh drops the comments and newlines, removes
# mount.usr=dissect from installer.cmdline, fills in the @...@ values and then appends systemd.image_policy=,
# systemd.image_filter= and console= copied from the main UKI, as for the full-disk installer).
# The initrd's mastersos-ventoy-attach.service checks the system file's version and size against these values before it
# attaches the file as a read-only loop disk (loop-ref mastersos-ventoy). The file's bytes are not hashed: the copy on the
# stick is trusted. A damaged copy is not detected at install time (the installer copies the /usr partitions block for block
# without checking them); it shows up later as I/O errors on the installed machine, fixed by reinstalling from a fresh copy.
# /usr is not found by gpt-auto (mount.usr=dissect needs no LoaderDevicePartUUID and the loop-ref "rootdisk"): the verity
# data and hash devices point explicitly at that loop disk's partitions, never at by-partuuid (an internal disk installed
# from the same version carries the same PARTUUIDs), and the root hash is the main UKI's signed usrhash=.
# veritysetup-generator (systemd v259) names the device systemd-veritysetup@usr -> /dev/mapper/usr.
mount.usr=/dev/mapper/usr
usrhash=@USRHASH@
systemd.verity_usr_data=/dev/disk/by-loop-ref/mastersos-ventoy-part4
systemd.verity_usr_hash=/dev/disk/by-loop-ref/mastersos-ventoy-part3
mastersos.ventoy=@VERSION@
mastersos.ventoy_size=@SIZE@
# usr-repair looks for a golden copy of the boot disk's /usr; the boot disk here is the Ventoy stick, never repair it
rd.systemd.mask=mastersos-usr-repair.service
